# Rules Maintenance

## When to Add New Rules
- When a new development pattern or best practice is established
- When a recurring issue is identified that could be prevented with guidelines
- When new technologies or tools are introduced to the project
- When team consensus is reached on a new standard
- When security or performance concerns require specific guidelines
- When regulatory requirements change
- When project scope or requirements change

## How to Update Rules
1. Create a new branch for the rules update
2. Document the reason for the change in the commit message
3. Update the relevant section(s) of this document
4. Update the README if the change affects the rules overview
5. Create a pull request for team review
6. Get approval from at least two team members
7. Merge the changes into main
8. Communicate changes to the team

## Rules Review Process
- Review rules quarterly or when major project changes occur
- Remove outdated or unnecessary rules
- Consolidate overlapping rules
- Ensure rules remain relevant to current project needs
- Update examples and references as needed
- Document review decisions
- Communicate changes to the team

## Rule Exceptions
- Document any exceptions to rules in the relevant section
- Include the reason for the exception
- Review exceptions during quarterly reviews
- Remove exceptions when they are no longer needed
- Document exception approval process
- Monitor exception usage
- Review exception impact

## Rules Documentation
- Keep rules documentation up-to-date
- Use clear, concise language
- Include examples where helpful
- Document rule rationale
- Include references to related rules
- Document rule dependencies
- Keep documentation organized

## Rules Communication
- Communicate rule changes to the team
- Document rule change history
- Provide training on new rules
- Gather feedback on rules
- Address rule-related questions
- Monitor rule compliance
- Review rule effectiveness
